[center][size=6][b]YADS — Yet Another Digital Storage Mod[/b][/size]
[size=3][i]Network storage for Fields of Mistria. One search bar for every crate you own.[/i][/size]

[b]Beta 1.0[/b] · by mykay · requires [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fieldsofmistria/mods/78]Mods of Mistria Installer[/url] 0.15.1+[/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]💾 What is this?[/b][/size]

If you have ever played modded Minecraft or Terraria, you already know this mod. YADS is a love letter to [b]Applied Energistics 2[/b], [b]Refined Storage[/b] and [b]Magic Storage[/b] — the "stop opening forty chests" genre — rebuilt for Mistria's cozy pace. It is, proudly, yet another digital storage mod.

Link storage units into a network, walk up to one terminal, and see [i]everything[/i]: searchable, sortable, filterable, on pages, with quick-stack. Your hoard, one screen.

[size=5][b]📦 The three units[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Storage Heart[/b] — [i]50 Wood · 5 Copper Ingot · 1 Glass[/i]
The brain. Every network needs exactly one. Interact for a status report: how many blocks are connected, how full each one is, how full the network is. The heart doesn't store your things — it knows where they are.
[*][b]Storage Block[/b] — [i]30 Wood · 1 Copper Ingot[/i]
30 slots of network capacity each. Chain as many as you like. Sealed while connected (the network handles them); a block with no heart behaves as a plain chest, so your items are never trapped.
[*][b]Access Panel[/b] — [i]1 Copper Ingot · 1 Glass[/i]
The terminal. Interact to browse the whole network.
[/list]

All three are crafted at the [b]Woodcrafting Station[/b] from crafting level 1 — a brand-new save qualifies, and the station is pre-placed on every farm. They list in [b]two[/b] places: under vanilla's own "Chests & Storage", and under a dedicated [b]"Digital Storage"[/b] sub-category (Functional tab, last in the list) so the set stays together as it grows. 日本語 players get a translated header for it.

[b]⚠ Copper [i]Ingot[/i], not Copper Ore.[/b] Ingots are refined at a [b]Forge[/b] — a Blacksmithing-discipline placeable sold by the Carpenter for 2,000g, and [i]not[/i] pre-placed on a new farm. Balor's Wagon also rotates Copper Ingot into stock sometimes. Budget for that before you go mining.

[size=5][b]✨ New in Beta 1.0[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Squeeze between your crates.[/b] Each unit now blocks you only on its solid core, with a walkable margin either side — so a long shelf of Blocks is a wall you can still walk along instead of a fence you have to go around. The units still feel solid when you walk into them; you just stop getting stuck behind your own storage.
[*][b]Value badges sit higher.[/b] Two pixels up and clear of the item art, so the number reads against the slot instead of against a sprite.
[*][b]Softer glow.[/b] The connection and fill colours are pastel now — the same green / yellow / red reading, in Mistria's own palette rather than in neon.
[/list]

[b]⚠ Updating from an Alpha build:[/b] the mod's internal id changed with the rename, so the installer treats this as a new mod. Delete the old [font=Courier New]mods/Digital Storage/[/font] folder, extract the new [font=Courier New]mods/yads/[/font], and [b]tick YADS once in the MOMI list[/b] — the old entry's tick doesn't carry over. Your saves and everything stored in placed units are untouched: the item and object names deliberately did not change. Two settings reset once — auto-search (back to on) and the value-badge mode (back to off).

[size=5][b]🔌 Building a network[/b][/size]

Place units so their footprints touch on an edge — heart, blocks, panel, in any arrangement, like one long shelf or a solid slab. Connected units [b]glow[/b]; a unit that missed the chain shows a little [b]sad face[/b] until you nudge it into place. One heart per chain is required (more is fine).

Blocks tint their glow by how full they are: [b]green[/b] empty, [b]yellow[/b] in use, [b]red[/b] full — so you can eyeball capacity from across the farm. Hearts and Panels stay plain cyan; they aren't deposit targets, so they have no fill level to report.

Crafting stations already pull materials from chests in vanilla Mistria — so everything in your network [b]feeds every recipe automatically[/b]. No cables, no export bus. Caldarus would call it magic; we call it a warehouse.

[size=5][b]🔎 The network view[/b][/size]

Open an Access Panel and you get the vanilla storage UI, 45 item cells a page, over every connected unit at once.

[list]
[*][b]A real search box.[/b] Click to place the cursor, then type — arrows, Home/End, Backspace/Delete, Shift+select, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+C/X/V, the lot. With auto-search on (it's a toggle, and it persists) the box is already focused the moment the panel opens, AE2-style: walk up, type "iron", done.
[*][b]16 category filters[/b] on a paged bottom row — All, the 14 item categories, and [b]"Museum needs"[/b], which shows only the items the museum is still missing. One filter at a time, and it narrows down further with whatever is in the search box.
[*][b]Value badges.[/b] A small coin-and-number in the corner of every occupied cell, cycling [b]off → per stack → per item[/b] (your choice persists). It's what selling would actually pay you right now — quality, infusions and perks included — and big totals abbreviate, so [i]12.3k[/i] instead of a number that eats the cell.
[*][b]Five sort modes[/b], cycled from one button: [b]Category → Name → Value → Stack val → Count[/b]. "Stack val" ranks by what the whole stack is worth rather than one item, which is the number you actually want when deciding what to sell.
[*][b]Pages[/b] via the arrows on the bottom bar, the mouse wheel over the grid, or PAGE_UP / PAGE_DOWN.
[*][b]Quick-stack to network[/b] from the banner button: every backpack stack whose kind the network already holds gets pushed in, from any page.
[*]If a deposit can't fully fit, the remainder comes straight back to you — backpack, or your feet if that's full too — with a "Network storage is full." toast. Nothing is ever silently eaten.
[/list]

[size=5][b]⛏️ Removal protection[/b][/size]

Swinging a pickaxe at a unit that still holds items doesn't take it apart on the first hit. The first swing shows a one-line toast and vanilla's own "that didn't work" shake-and-rumble; it takes [b]five swings on the same unit[/b] to actually break it, and it resets after ten seconds of not swinging, on reload, or on leaving the room. A wide charged swing counts as [b]one[/b], not one per tile it covers. So does a bomb blast. It works the same from every side, including while you are standing on the unit.

This is a guard against the [i]accident[/i] — the swing aimed at the rock behind the crate — and it is sized for exactly that. It is not data-loss prevention, because there was never any data loss to prevent: vanilla already drops a broken unit's entire inventory on the ground, same as any chest. What it saves you is the afternoon spent picking fifty-four stacks back up.

Two things still get through it, both engine-level: erasing a unit under a [b]blueprint or farm expansion[/b] (that path never reaches the pickaxe code at all), and a unit standing [b]on a table[/b] (the swing resolves the table, which comes apart in one hit and takes its whole surface with it). Contents drop to the ground either way.

[size=5][b]🛡️ Your items are safe (the boring engineering bit)[/b][/size]

This mod is built around one rule: [b]it never holds your items.[/b] Everything you store lives in the real chest inventories of the placed units, which the game's own save system writes — the big network view is a live mirror, reconciled every frame. If the game crashes with the view open, nothing is lost.

The mod adds nothing of its own to your save file. The removal guard is the one place it touches a field the game does save, and it is handled accordingly: the value is set for the length of a single swing, put back on the very next frame, and put back again before the game writes a single byte — so an uninstalled mod leaves your crates exactly as vanilla made them.

It was built against the game's engine source with an adversarial review process specifically hunting item-loss bugs, because a storage mod that eats items isn't a storage mod, it's a shredder with UI.

[b]⚠ The one warning that matters:[/b] before UNINSTALLING the mod, empty your storage units. The engine silently discards placed objects it no longer recognises — along with their contents. (Items in your backpack survive fine.)

[size=5][b]🧪 Beta notes[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Works on existing saves — recipes unlock automatically on load, no popup.
[*]Networks are per-room (engine grids are per-location). Farm and farmhouse are separate networks; each farmhouse room too.
[*]Don't place units on tables — table surfaces are separate sub-grids, won't link, and get no swing protection.
[*][b]You can wall yourself in with the new walkable margins — and you can always get out.[/b] Stand in the gap between two flush units, box it in with more units above and below, and you're sealed in a one-tile pocket (vanilla's placement check never tested for enclosure either). Swing your pickaxe at any adjacent unit to break out — one swing if it's empty, five if it holds items. Sleeping or leaving the room also fixes it.
[*]Deposits go to Blocks only, never the Heart or Panel. A heart-only network has nowhere to put anything; add a Block.
[*]A Heart on a network with no Access Panel yet just tells you to craft one — no status popup until there's a way in.
[*]The vanilla [i]Throw[/i] key can still stuff an item into any specific unit directly — engine behavior no mod hook can intercept. Harmless; the network view shows items wherever they land.
[*]Sealed blocks still show the vanilla "Open" prompt before politely refusing — same story, the prompt is engine-side.
[*]The category row stays one row of 8 at any resolution: the menu renders on a fixed logical canvas, so a bigger monitor doesn't buy more room.
[*]Back up your saves before installing any mod. Good practice, not a warning about this one.
[/list]

[size=5][b]📥 Install[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Extract into your [font=Courier New]Fields of Mistria/mods/[/font] folder (so it reads [font=Courier New]mods/yads/manifest.json[/font] — no double-nesting). [b]Keep the folder named [font=Courier New]yads[/font][/b]; the installer derives the mod's code namespace from it.
[*]Run the Mods of Mistria Installer, tick [b]YADS[/b] → Install.
[*]Play. Craft a Heart, chain some Blocks, add a Panel, never open forty chests again.
[/list]

